#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Check an oh-my-pi branch for what roboomp keeps ranking down, before the PR is marked ready.

usage: precheck [N] [--ref REF] [--body FILE] [--number N] [--title T] [--no-siblings] [--base REF]
  N              PR number on the upstream repo: checks <fork remote>/<its branch> plus its body and links
                 (fetches that branch first). Without N: checks HEAD of the worktree you run it in,
                 and skips the body checks.
  --ref REF      check REF in the repo you run it in instead of HEAD (pr-queue: a queued branch)
  --body FILE    run the body checks on FILE, for a PR not opened yet
  --number N     the PR number the CHANGELOG entries must link, with --ref (no GitHub calls)
  --no-siblings  skip the checks against our other open PRs (conflicts, carried commits)
  --title T      the PR title the commits' scopes are checked against (pr-queue: the entry's
                 title); with N it is the PR's, else the oldest commit's subject
  --base REF     diff against REF instead of origin/main: a queued branch whose needs have not
                 merged (its recorded base), or an old commit replayed against its original base

FIX lines are what roboomp flags as should-fix or blocking; fix them before marking ready (or
before the next push). NOTE lines need a decision, not necessarily a change. Exit status 1 when
any FIX line printed. Checks:
  - inline `import(...)` and `as never` / `as any` escapes in added TypeScript lines (FIX), and
    other new `as` casts in non-test source (NOTE: keep one only where it narrows at an IO or
    page-evaluation boundary; cleanup.md removes casts that paper over flow)
  - `expect(...).rejects` in added test lines (NOTE: when the promise settles from I/O, use
    `rejectionOf` from test/helpers/rejection.ts; `.rejects` hangs Bun on Windows, and CI runs no
    Windows tests)
  - wall-clock tests in added test lines (FIX): sleeps, clock reads, elapsed-time assertions and
    timer races (`Bun.sleep`, `performance.now`, `setTimeout(…, ms)`, `AbortSignal.timeout`,
    `thread::sleep`, `.elapsed()`) pass or fail by machine load and fail CI;
    `Bun.sleep(0)` (one timer turn) and a Rust `Instant::now()` epoch the test offsets explicitly
    are fine; fake timers in the file exempt timer and elapsed lines; a per-test time limit is a NOTE
  - repo tooling on what the diff adds (FIX): oxlint findings on added TS lines (`bun check` passes
    on warnings, so `unicorn/no-thenable` went unflagged on #15274 until a maintainer annotated it), files
    oxfmt would reformat (#15125, committed after the last check), and `new Promise(` outside a
    string (AGENTS.md wants `Promise.withResolvers()`; the maintainer rewrote it on #15118)
  - every package whose source the diff touches has an added line under `## [Unreleased]` in its
    CHANGELOG.md (crates/pi-natives counts as packages/natives); with N, each added entry links the
    PR and credits @<you> (`github.fork_owner`). Skipped when every commit is `test:` (AGENTS.md: entries are
    user-facing, and inline Rust test modules live under src/). Where the diff rewrites source lines
    from commits no `v*` tag ships, the fix may be of a regression no user had: a missing entry is
    a NOTE, and an added one a NOTE naming those commits (the maintainer dropped #15941's, `7c9d457d70`)
  - the same entries after a simulated merge of origin/main (FIX): merge=union leaves an entry
    under a release cut since the branch point, or repeats a `###` heading. The maintainer asks
    for this on every PR; rebase onto origin/main and move the line
  - tests maintainers pared from our merged PRs (NOTE, code-style.md *Count tests by code path*):
    `log-test`, a test spying on or mocking the repo logger (#16579, `c98d4561f0`); `prompt-test`,
    an assertion that a `src/prompts/*.md` file's text (imported or read) appears in a description,
    prompt or request, or a test case building a full `createAgentSession` whose only assertions are
    `toContain`/`toMatch` on prompt or description text (#16918, `4b13432524`; a unit test of a
    description builder's section in or out is fine); `tests`, test volume over the
    change's: added test lines over 1.5x the added source lines by 20 or more, or more than 3 added
    test cases (`it(`, `test(`, each `.each` row, Rust `#[test]`) per changed source file.
    CHANGELOGs, docs, generated and lock files, fixtures and snapshots count as neither
  - test shapes maintainers rewrote (NOTE): full-string assertions, `test.each` rows (each must
    fail with the fix reverted), casts in mocks, and new tests beside credential code that falls
    back to env keys
  - changed parameter lists on package source declarations (NOTE: Breaking Changes entry if
    exported), and docs naming a changed function that the diff leaves untouched (NOTE)
  - what the diff takes out or adds beside its one change (NOTE, code-style.md): each call
    statement it removes from source and adds back nowhere (#15604 dropped
    `enable_web_accessibility`), a commit whose conventional scope is outside the title's (#15414's
    `test(coding-agent)` commit on a `fix(natives)` PR), a test asserting on script or source
    text instead of running it (#15599's cmux test), and source files that name a third of a
    type file's fields but not a field the diff adds to it (#15680's anonymizer)
  - model or provider policy in TypeScript outside packages/catalog (NOTE): a new numeric constant
    whose name, line or doc comment names a provider or model family, and string matching on a
    model id (`id.includes(...)`). AGENTS.md puts that policy in the catalog's KDL rules; the maintainer moved
    it there on #11613, #8577, #14286 and #14751
  - a credential-shaped value (a name or expression with url, token, apiKey, auth, header, env, …)
    passed unredacted to `prompt.render(...)`, or rendered by a prompt template the diff adds
    (NOTE): the prompt goes to the model provider; #16390 rendered `browser.cdpUrl` and the maintainer
    redacted it (914654c252)
  - a change that can move agent behaviour (NOTE, advisory): prompt text, or tool, prelude or eval
    code the model calls (`src/prompts/`, `src/tools/`, `src/eval/`), whose `## Testing` names no
    sample-set run on two models of different families (`models.behaviour` in your config; unset,
    any two families). They read the same line differently (#15402: a GPT model ignored a todo rule
    Opus kept); #16231 rewrote the todo
    rule every model reads and was checked only by tests that render the text. A change no model
    sees (a parser fix, a cleanup) needs none: the NOTE is a prompt to decide
  - Rust (FIX; NOTE when `build.<platform>` is "none" or its ssh host does not answer): a branch
    changing Rust needs `rust-check` recorded (the queue manifest's
    `rust_checks`, fetched) for its Rust diff on Linux, plus macOS or Windows when it touches code
    under that OS's paths or `cfg`; CI compiles no Rust before the merge (#16202's Wayland E0618,
    bf9fd4bd6a; #16140's macOS clippy, ef7c45bd4b)
  - the independent review (workflow step 3, recorded with `review-stamp`): NOTE when none is
    recorded, and when it asked for changes (each must-fix fixed or answered; no second round)
  - commit messages naming a PR as `#N`, `owner/repo#N` or a PR URL (NOTE): each push of a new SHA
    lands a reference on that PR's timeline; `quiet-refs` rewrites them after the next rebase the
    PR needs anyway, never in a push of its own
  - body (with N or --body): What/Why/Testing headings, each with text, the own-words line opening `## What`
    (FIX unless that first paragraph is exactly a sentence recorded with `contrib record own-words`
    for this branch, whitespace aside: the user's words, never a substitute; a branch with no
    contribution record on this host gets the first-person check instead), under 3,000 characters
    outside tables, <details>, images and the checklist, no @-mention of
    Codex, roboomp or a maintainer, and no merge-order line (`Builds on #N` for an open PR of ours,
    `merge #N first`, `Merge after`, `Overlaps #N`; FIX: shaping.md, Open-PR rule)
  - queue (with N, FIX): a closed PR whose change `pr-queue` holds queued reopens with the stored
    body, so an edit to GitHub's is lost (queue.md); and a draft or unopened PR while the queue's
    opening pause is set (`pr-queue pause`): it stays as it is until `pr-queue unpause`
  - links (with N): open issues the body mentions that the PR does not close, so each is a
    deliberate Fixes-or-Related call; and the issues it does close
  - siblings (FIX, shaping.md Open-PR rule): commits of another open PR of ours on this branch
    (by patch id, so older copies count), and a code conflict with an open ready PR of ours once
    both land on origin/main. NOTE instead for a code conflict the rule accepts: a P0/P1 entry and
    an open P2 open more than 3 days, or a pair `pr-queue open` opened that way (Open-PR rule 3);
    NOTE for a conflict only in the generated catalog files, which the
    maintainer regenerates at merge, and for each crate or package this branch changes that an open
    ready PR of ours changes too (crates/<name>, packages/<name>): git merges the two cleanly, yet
    the pair can fail to build (#15761 + #15762, E0507), so build and test the merged tree

A run without N on a local branch records its result for that branch (sha, pass or FIX, the body
checked), which `contrib next` and `pr-queue add` read: the queue takes a branch only once precheck
passed at its tip with the body it queues.
"""
import argparse
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))
import omp_config  # noqa: E402
import omp_flow  # noqa: E402
from omp_pr import CHECKOUT, CODE, MAINTAINERS, ME, REPO, TS, Diff, blamed_commits, changelog_drift, gh_json, last_review, run  # noqa: E402
from omp_pr import is_test_path, package_of, rust_checks, rust_diff_id, rust_platforms  # noqa: E402
from omp_queue import (STORE_REMOTE, GitError, GitHub, Queue, Store, merge_order_lines, opening_pause, pause_line,  # noqa: E402
                       quiet_refs, stored_body_entry, stored_body_message)

BODY_LIMIT = 3000
MENTIONS = re.compile(r"@(" + "|".join(map(re.escape, ("codex", "chatgpt-codex-connector", "roboomp", *MAINTAINERS)))
                      + r")\b", re.I)
CAST = re.compile(r"""[\w)\]}>"'`]\s+as\s+(?!const\b)[\w{\[(<]""")
ESCAPE_CAST = re.compile(r"\bas\s+(never|any)\b")
INLINE_IMPORT = re.compile(r"\bimport\s*\(")
REJECTS = re.compile(r"\)\s*\.rejects\b")
STRING = re.compile(r"""("(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*"|'(?:[^'\\]|\\.)*'|`(?:[^`\\]|\\.)*`)""")
LONG_STRING = re.compile(r"""^\s*["'`].{40,}""")
PROMISE = re.compile(r"\bnew\s+Promise\s*[<(]")
# The globs `check:tools` hands to oxfmt.
FMT_SCOPE = re.compile(r"^(packages/[^/]+/src/.+\.tsx?|packages/[^/]+/(test|bench|examples|scripts)/.+\.ts|"
                       r"packages/[^/]+/[^/]+\.ts|scripts/.+\.ts)$")
OX_LINE = re.compile(r"^(.+?):(\d+):\d+: (.*)$")
EXACT_ASSERT = re.compile(r"""\.(toBe|toEqual|toStrictEqual)\(\s*(["'`].{40,})?$""")
MOCK_CAST = re.compile(r"\bas\s+typeof\s+\w+")
# A test whose result depends on how fast the machine runs: a loaded CI box fails it and an idle one
# passes, so it is flaky by construction. Fake timers or an injected clock make
# time an input; awaiting the event itself replaces a sleep. `Bun.sleep(0)` crosses one timer turn
# without waiting for time to pass, so it stays allowed.
TS_CLOCK = re.compile(r"\bBun\.sleep(?:Sync)?\s*\((?!\s*0\s*\))|\bperformance\.now\s*\(|\bprocess\.hrtime\b"
                      r"|\bBun\.nanoseconds\s*\(|\bAbortSignal\.timeout\s*\(|\bwithTimeout\s*\(")
TS_TIMER = re.compile(r"\bsetTimeout\s*\(.*,\s*(?:[1-9][\d_]*|[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{2,})\s*\)")
TS_ELAPSED = re.compile(r"(?:(?i:elapsed|duration|took|latency|millis)|[a-z]Ms\b|_ms\b|\bms\b|Date\.now\s*\(\))"
                        r".*\.toBe(?:Less|Greater)Than")
TS_TEST_LIMIT = re.compile(r"^\s*\}\s*,\s*(?:\d[\d_]*|\{\s*timeout\s*:)")
FAKE_CLOCK = re.compile(r"\b(?:useFakeTimers|setSystemTime|advanceTimersByTime|advanceTimersToNextTimer|runAllTimers"
                        r"|runOnlyPendingTimers)\b")
# `let t0 = Instant::now();` alone is an epoch: the test hands the code `t0 + ms(n)` and nothing reads
# the clock again. Reading it to compare, subtract or measure is timing.
RS_CLOCK = re.compile(r"\bthread::sleep\s*\(|\.elapsed\s*\(|\bInstant::now\s*\(\)\s*(?:[-<>]|\.duration_since)"
                      r"|[-<>]=?\s*Instant::now\s*\(|\brecv_timeout\s*\(|\btime::(?:sleep|timeout)\s*\(")
# Rust test code starts at a `#[cfg(test)]` module; the same attribute on one item (a test-only
# constructor in production code, #16284's skylight.rs) does not make what follows a test.
RS_TEST_MOD = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:pub(?:\([\w ]+\))?\s+)?mod\s")
EACH = re.compile(r"\b(test|it|describe)\.each\b")
# A declaration line: `export function f(a)`, `async f(a): T {`, `f(a) {`. Single-line params only.
DECL = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:export\s+)?(?:(?:public|static|async|function)\s+)*([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\s*\(([^()]*)\)\s*[:{]")
NOT_DECL = {"if", "for", "while", "switch", "catch", "return", "await", "function", "new", "typeof"}
CREDENTIAL_SRC = re.compile(r"packages/ai/src/(auth|auth-broker|registry/oauth|providers|usage)/")
DOC_GLOBS = ("docs/*.md", "docs/**/*.md", "packages/*/docs/*.md", "packages/*/docs/**/*.md")
# Identifier-shaped names only (camelCase, PascalCase, snake_case): `inherit` matches half the docs.
DOC_NAME = re.compile(r"[a-z0-9][A-Z]|_[a-z]")
# The enclosing function git prints after a hunk's `@@` (`export function f(`, `  async m(`). Class
# and interface names are left out: a change inside `AuthStorage` is not a change to every doc naming it.
CONTEXT_NAME = re.compile(
    r"\bfunction\s+([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)|^\s*(?:(?:export|async|static|public)\s+)*([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\s*\(")
NUM_CONST = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:export\s+)?const\s+[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*\s*(?::\s*[\w.]+\s*)?=\s*-?\d[\d_.]*\s*;?\s*(//.*)?$")
PROVIDER_WORD = re.compile(
    r"(?i)(anthropic|claude|openai|gpt-?\d|gemini|vertex|bedrock|cursor|devin|kimi|moonshot|grok|xai\b|"
    r"deepseek|mistral|copilot|codex|antigravity|minimax|glm|qwen|fireworks|openrouter|opus|sonnet|haiku)")
ID_MATCH = re.compile(r"\bid\??\.(includes|startsWith|endsWith|match)\(")
POLICY_LOOKBACK = 8
# A provider's own transport, login and usage modules hold that provider's wire constants and id
# formats; policy shared across models is what belongs in KDL.
PROVIDER_MODULE = re.compile(r"^packages/ai/src/(providers|registry/oauth|usage)/")
# A statement that is only a call, its result unused: `foo(a);`, `await x.y(b);`, `mod::f(&c);`, or
# the first line of a call spanning several. Macros (`println!(`) are left out.
CALL_STMT = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:await\s+|void\s+|let\s+_\s*=\s*)?(?:(?:[A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\s*(?:\?\.|\.|::|->)\s*)*"
                       r"([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\s*\(")
NOT_CALL = {"if", "for", "while", "switch", "catch", "return", "await", "function", "new", "typeof", "elif",
            "match", "with", "fn", "def", "assert", "pub", "impl", "struct", "enum", "trait", "type"}
# Not a call: a method signature (`text(s: string): Promise<T>;`).
NOT_CALL_SHAPE = re.compile(r"\)\s*:")
# An inline Rust test module, by the hunk context git prints (`mod capture_tests {`).
TEST_MODULE = re.compile(r"\bmod\s+\w*tests?\b")
STMT_END = (";", "(", "{")
# Any callee in a line: a removed call whose callee an added line still calls was moved or reworded.
CALLEE = re.compile(r"([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\s*(?:::<[^>]*>\s*)?\(")
# A test asserting on a script's or a source file's text rather than on what running it does.
SCRIPT_ASSERT = re.compile(r"\bexpect\(\s*(?:[\w$]*[Ss]cript[\w$]*(?:\[[^\]]*\])?|(?:[\w$]+\.)*(?:src|source|sourceText|"
                           r"sourceCode)|[\w$.]+\.toString\(\))\s*\)\s*\.(?:not\.)?(?:toContain|toMatch|toInclude|"
                           r"toStartWith|toEndWith)\(")
SOURCE_READ = re.compile(r"""(?:readFileSync|readFile|Bun\.file|include_str!)\s*\([^)]*?["'`][^"'`]*"""
                         r"""\.(?:ts|tsx|js|mjs|rs|py)["'`]""")
# Tests maintainers pared from our merged PRs (code-style.md *Count tests by code path*). A spy on or
# a mock of the repo logger pins debug-log wording (#16579, `c98d4561f0`). Prompt wording: on #16918
# H4vC deleted the SDK test that built a whole `createAgentSession` to find a `computer.md` line in
# its first request, and kept the unit test of the description builder's new branch, which checks a
# section in or out (`4b13432524`). So the NOTE is for an assertion that a prompt file's text shows
# up somewhere, and for a test that builds a full session to assert only on prompt text.
COUNT_RULE = "code-style.md *Count tests by code path*"
LOGGER_TEST = re.compile(r"\bspyOn\(\s*logger\s*,|\blogger\.(?:debug|info|warn|error)\s*(?:=[^=]|\.mock)"
                         r"|\bexpect\(\s*logger\.(?:debug|info|warn|error)\s*\)")
TEXT_MATCHER = re.compile(r"\s*\.(?:not\.)?(?:toContain|toMatch|toInclude|toStartWith|toEndWith)\(")
PROMPT_NAME = re.compile(r"(?:description|prompt|instructions)s?$", re.I)
PROMPT_FILE = re.compile(r"""["'`][^"'`]*\bprompts/[^"'`]*\.md["'`]""")
# A name bound to a prompt file's text: `import guide from "../src/prompts/tools/computer.md"`,
# `const guide = readFileSync(".../prompts/x.md", "utf8")`.
PROMPT_BINDING = re.compile(r"""^\s*(?:import\s+([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\s+from|(?:const|let|var)\s+([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\s*="""
                            r"""[^;]*?(?:readFileSync|readFile|Bun\.file))\s*[^;]*?["'`][^"'`]*\bprompts/[^"'`]*\.md["'`]""")
FULL_SESSION = re.compile(r"\bcreateAgentSession\s*\(|\bnew\s+AgentSession\s*\(")
# Added test cases: `it(`/`test(` (with `.only`, `.skip`, `.if(…)` and the like), each row of an
# `it.each`/`test.each` table, Rust `#[test]`/`#[tokio::test]`, Python `def test_`.
TEST_CASE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:it|test)(?:\.(?:only|skip|todo|concurrent|serial|failing)|\.(?:if|skipIf|runIf)"
                       r"\([^()]*\))*\s*\(|^\s*#\[(?:tokio::)?test\b|^\s*(?:async\s+)?def\s+test_")
EACH_CASE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:it|test)(?:\.\w+)*\.each\s*\(")
# Test-volume thresholds: test lines over TEST_LINE_RATIO times the source lines and by at least
# TEST_LINE_EXCESS lines (a 20-line regression test for a 5-line fix is the one test the rule
# wants), or more than CASES_PER_FILE cases per changed source file. On our 63 merges 2026-10-22..
# 11-02 this fires on 10 of the 13 PRs whose tests maintainers pared and 18 of the other 50; the
# excess floor drops 5 small PRs and one pared PR (#17772, whose pared test pinned a cache-key
# version, not volume). The two it misses (#16309, #17766) lost data-echo tests, not volume.
TEST_LINE_RATIO = 1.5
TEST_LINE_EXCESS = 20
CASES_PER_FILE = 3
# Neither tests nor source when counting: fixtures and snapshots, generated and lock files, docs.
NOT_COUNTED = re.compile(r"(^|/)(fixtures?|__snapshots__|testdata)/|\.snap$|(^|/)CHANGELOG\.md$|(^|/)docs/"
                         r"|(^|/)(models|rules)\.json$|(^|/)index\.d\.ts$|(^|/)native/index\.js$|(^|/)(bun\.lock|"
                         r"Cargo\.lock|package-lock\.json)$")
# A property in an interface or object type body; its hunk context names the type.
FIELD_DECL = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:readonly\s+)?([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\??\s*:\s*[^=]+?[;,]?\s*$")
FIELD_NAME = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:readonly\s+)?([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\??\s*:", re.M)
TYPE_CTX = re.compile(r"\binterface\s+\w|\btype\s+\w+(?:<[^>]*>)?\s*=")
# A type file whose fields a consumer can be judged against: fewer distinctive field names and a
# third of them is too few to tell an allowlist from a passing mention.
CONSUMER_MIN_FIELDS = 9
def rust_where(platform, placement=None):
    """(how to run rust-check for `platform`, or None when nothing checks it) from `build.<platform>`
    (references/config.md): "local", "ssh:<host>", "ci" or "none"."""
    placement = placement if placement is not None else omp_config.load().build[platform]
    flag = " --windows" if platform == "windows" else ""
    if placement == "local":
        return f"scripts/rust-check{flag} in the PR's worktree here"
    if placement.startswith("ssh:"):
        host = placement[4:]
        return f"scripts/rust-check{flag} in the PR's worktree on {host} (`ssh {host}`)"
    if placement == "ci":
        return f"scripts/rust-check --ci --platform {platform} (GitHub Actions on your fork)"
    return None


def host_reachable(host):
    """Whether `ssh host` answers within a few seconds without a prompt."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run(["ssh", "-o", "BatchMode=yes", "-o", "ConnectTimeout=5", host, "true"],
                              capture_output=True, timeout=15).returncode == 0
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return False
# Text a prompt template or `prompt.render` argument carries to the model provider. Words of a name
# (camelCase or snake_case split) that say the value can hold a credential: a URL's userinfo and
# query (#16390 rendered `browser.cdpUrl` verbatim; the maintainer wrapped it in redactUrlForLog, 914654c252).
SECRET_WORDS = {"url", "urls", "uri", "endpoint", "token", "tokens", "secret", "secrets", "password", "passwd",
                "apikey", "auth", "authorization", "cookie", "cookies", "credential", "credentials", "dsn",
                "header", "headers", "env"}
TEMPLATE_VAR = re.compile(r"\{\{\s*([A-Za-z_][\w.]*)\s*\}\}")
RENDER_CALL = re.compile(r"\bprompt\.render\(")
RENDER_ARG = re.compile(r"^\s*([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\s*:\s*(.+?),?\s*$")
RENDER_LOOKBACK = 15

findings = []


def fix(kind, where, detail=""):
    findings.append(1)
    print(f"FIX   {kind:<10} {where}  {detail}".rstrip())


def note(kind, where, detail=""):
    print(f"NOTE  {kind:<10} {where}  {detail}".rstrip())


def code_part(text):
    """The line with string contents blanked and any // comment removed; '' for comment lines."""
    stripped = text.strip()
    if stripped.startswith(("//", "*", "/*")):
        return ""
    code = STRING.sub(lambda m: m.group(0)[0] * 2, text)
    return code.split("//", 1)[0]


def base_has_inline_import(path, base, cwd):
    """True when `path` at `base` already loads modules with `import(` in code (a lazy-loader registry)."""
    blob = subprocess.run(["git", "show", f"{base}:{path}"], capture_output=True, text=True, cwd=cwd)
    return blob.returncode == 0 and any(INLINE_IMPORT.search(code_part(line)) for line in blob.stdout.splitlines())


def check_code(added, base=None, cwd=None):
    for path, lines in sorted(added.items()):
        if not path.endswith(TS):
            continue
        is_test = is_test_path(path)
        for i, (n, text) in enumerate(lines):
            code = code_part(text)
            if re.search(r"\b(import|export)\s+\*\s+as\b", code):
                continue
            if is_test and REJECTS.search(code):
                note("rejects", f"{path}:{n}", "I/O-settled promise? use rejectionOf (test/helpers/rejection.ts)")
            if ESCAPE_CAST.search(code):
                fix("cast", f"{path}:{n}", text.strip()[:100])
            elif is_test and MOCK_CAST.search(code):
                note("mock-cast", f"{path}:{n}", "cast in a mock: mockResolvedValue / a typed fixture needs none")
            elif CAST.search(code) and not is_test:
                note("cast", f"{path}:{n}", text.strip()[:100])
            if INLINE_IMPORT.search(code):
                if base and base_has_inline_import(path, base, cwd):
                    note("import()", f"{path}:{n}", "follows the file's existing import() loaders on main; say so in the body")
                else:
                    fix("import()", f"{path}:{n}", text.strip()[:100])
            if is_test:
                check_test_line(path, lines, i, n, text)


def check_test_line(path, lines, i, n, text):
    """Test shapes maintainers rewrote on our PRs (2026-10-08 round: #14763, #14765, #14753)."""
    m = EXACT_ASSERT.search(text.rstrip())
    following = lines[i + 1][1] if i + 1 < len(lines) and lines[i + 1][0] == n + 1 else ""
    if m and (m.group(2) or LONG_STRING.match(following)):
        note("exact-str", f"{path}:{n}", "full-string assertion: keep only if a consumer parses these bytes")
    if EACH.search(code_part(text)):
        note("each", f"{path}:{n}", "every row must fail with the fix reverted and cover a distinct case")


def file_at(ref, path, cwd):
    shown = run("git", "show", f"{ref}:{path}", cwd=cwd)
    return shown.stdout if shown.returncode == 0 else ""


def check_timing(added, ref, cwd):
    """Added test lines whose pass or fail depends on wall-clock time (FIX): sleeps, clock reads,
    elapsed-time assertions and timer races. A per-test time limit is a NOTE. Rust test code is a
    file under a test path or anything below the file's `#[cfg(test)]`."""
    for path, lines in sorted(added.items()):
        if path.endswith(TS) and is_test_path(path):
            fake = None
            for n, text in lines:
                code = code_part(text)
                hit = TS_CLOCK.search(code)
                if not hit and (TS_TIMER.search(code) or TS_ELAPSED.search(code)):
                    if fake is None:
                        fake = bool(FAKE_CLOCK.search(file_at(ref, path, cwd)))
                    hit = not fake
                if hit:
                    fix("timing", f"{path}:{n}", "wall-clock test: use fake timers or an injected clock, "
                        "and await the event instead of sleeping")
                elif TS_TEST_LIMIT.match(code):
                    note("timing", f"{path}:{n}", "per-test time limit: never lower the runner default; a test "
                         "that needs a raised one is slow, not bounded")
        elif path.endswith(".rs"):
            start = 0 if is_test_path(path) else None
            for n, text in lines:
                code = code_part(text)
                if not RS_CLOCK.search(code):
                    continue
                if start is None:
                    start = rust_test_start(path, ref, cwd)
                if 0 <= start <= n:
                    fix("timing", f"{path}:{n}", "wall-clock test: inject the clock or wait on the event, "
                        "never sleep or time a call")


def rust_test_start(path, ref, cwd):
    """The line Rust test code starts at in `path` at `ref`: the line after the `#[cfg(test)]` that
    opens a test module, -1 when the file has none."""
    head = file_at(ref, path, cwd).splitlines()
    return next((i + 2 for i, line in enumerate(head[:-1]) if line.strip() == "#[cfg(test)]"
                 and RS_TEST_MOD.match(head[i + 1])), 0) or -1


def check_policy(added):
    """Model or provider policy written in TS instead of the catalog's KDL rules (AGENTS.md, "Model/
    Provider Policy Lives in KDL"). #14751 shipped `CACHE_LOOKBACK_POSITIONS = 20` under a comment
    about Anthropic and applied it to every model; the maintainer replaced it with a `prompt-cache-lookback`
    axis on the anthropic class (`1111b1fc03`)."""
    for path, lines in sorted(added.items()):
        if (not path.endswith(TS) or is_test_path(path) or path.startswith("packages/catalog/")
                or PROVIDER_MODULE.match(path)):
            continue
        for i, (n, text) in enumerate(lines):
            code = code_part(text)
            if ID_MATCH.search(code):
                note("kdl", f"{path}:{n}", "string match on a model id: policy belongs in the catalog KDL rules")
                continue
            if not NUM_CONST.match(text):
                continue
            # The constant's own line and the contiguous added lines just above it (its doc comment).
            block = [text]
            for prior_n, prior in reversed(lines[max(0, i - POLICY_LOOKBACK):i]):
                if prior_n != n - len(block):
                    break
                block.append(prior)
            word = next((m.group(1) for t in block if (m := PROVIDER_WORD.search(t))), None)
            if word:
                note("kdl", f"{path}:{n}", f"numeric constant tied to {word!r}: a catalog KDL axis, scoped "
                                           f"to the class it is true for? ({text.strip()[:60]})")


def check_review(branch, cwd):
    """The one workflow step 3 review round (`review-stamp`). Fixes after it are not re-reviewed
   : roboomp reviews the PR next."""
    review = last_review(branch, cwd)
    if not review:
        note("review", branch, "no independent review recorded: run the one review round (workflow step 3), "
                               "then scripts/review-stamp")
        return
    sha, verdict, at, by = review
    if verdict == "needs-changes":
        note("review", branch, f"the review at {sha[:10]} ({by or 'reviewer'}, {at}) asked for changes: each "
                               "must-fix fixed (behaviour fixes with a test that fails on revert) or answered")


def name_words(text):
    """Lower-case words of the identifiers and strings in `text`, camelCase and snake_case split,
    with `api` + `key` joined (`apiKey`, `API_KEY` -> apikey)."""
    words = [w.lower() for w in re.findall(r"[A-Z]?[a-z]+|[A-Z]+(?![a-z])|\d+", text)]
    return set(words) | {"apikey" for a, b in zip(words, words[1:]) if (a, b) == ("api", "key")}


def render_args(path, lines, ref, cwd):
    """[(line, name, expression)] of the added lines in `path` that are arguments of a
    `prompt.render(template, {...})` call: the nearest line above (within RENDER_LOOKBACK) indented
    less than the argument opens the call."""
    text = run("git", "show", f"{ref}:{path}", cwd=cwd).stdout.splitlines()
    found = []
    for n, line in lines:
        m = RENDER_ARG.match(code_part(line))
        if not m:
            continue
        indent = len(line) - len(line.lstrip())
        for above in reversed(text[max(0, n - 1 - RENDER_LOOKBACK):n - 1]):
            if above.strip() and len(above) - len(above.lstrip()) < indent:
                if RENDER_CALL.search(above):
                    found.append((n, m.group(1), (RENDER_ARG.match(line) or m).group(2)))
                break
    return found


def check_prompt_values(added, ref, cwd):
    """A settings or env value that can hold a credential (a URL with userinfo or a token query, a
    key, a header) rendered into model-visible text without redaction: the system prompt goes to
    the model provider. #16390 rendered `browser.cdpUrl` into the relay guidance block, and the maintainer
    redacted it (914654c252, `redactUrlForLog`)."""
    passed = []
    for path, lines in sorted(added.items()):
        if path.endswith(TS) and not is_test_path(path) and "/src/" in path:
            passed += [(name, f"{path}:{n}", expr) for n, name, expr in render_args(path, lines, ref, cwd)]
    # Each argument on its own: a redacted `url` at one call says nothing of another call's `url`.
    for name, where, expr in passed:
        if "redact" not in expr.lower() and (name_words(name) | name_words(expr)) & SECRET_WORDS:
            note("secret", where, f"`{name}: {expr.strip()[:60]}` goes into a prompt the model provider reads: "
                                  "can it carry a credential (URL userinfo or token query, key, header)? redact it "
                                  "(redactUrlForLog) or render only the safe part")
    # A placeholder whose value an added argument passes is judged at that argument, above.
    passed = {name for name, _, _ in passed}
    for path, lines in sorted(added.items()):
        if "/src/" not in path or not path.endswith(".md") or is_test_path(path):
            continue
        for n, text in lines:
            for name in TEMPLATE_VAR.findall(text):
                if name in passed or not name_words(name) & SECRET_WORDS:
                    continue
                note("secret", f"{path}:{n}", f"renders {{{{{name}}}}} into model-visible text: check the value its "
                                              "`prompt.render` caller passes can carry no credential, or redact it")


AGENT_FACING = re.compile(r"/src/(prompts/.+\.md|tools/.+|eval/.+)$")
# Model families a Testing section may name, for when `models.behaviour` is not configured.
FAMILIES = {"Anthropic": re.compile(r"\b(haiku|sonnet|opus|claude)\b", re.I),
            "OpenAI": re.compile(r"\b(gpt-\d[\w.-]*|codex|o\d(?:-mini)?)\b", re.I),
            "Google": re.compile(r"\bgemini\b", re.I), "xAI": re.compile(r"\bgrok\b", re.I),
            "open-weight": re.compile(r"\b(kimi|glm|deepseek|qwen|llama|mistral|minimax)\b", re.I)}


def model_pattern(model):
    """A model id (`anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5`, `gpt-5.1-codex`) as Testing would name it: its last
    path segment, case-insensitive; a thinking suffix (`:low`) is not part of the name."""
    name = omp_config.strip_thinking(model).rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
    return re.compile(r"(?<![\w.-])" + re.escape(name) + r"(?![\w.-])", re.I)


def check_behavior_eval(added, body):
    """What agents do with a prompt or a tool is measured by running them, and two model families read
    the same line differently. The two models are `models.behaviour` in your config; unset, any two
    families named will do. Advisory: the path only hints the change can reach the model."""
    paths = sorted(p for p in added if AGENT_FACING.search(p) and not is_test_path(p))
    if not paths:
        return
    where = paths[0] + (f" (+{len(paths) - 1} more)" if len(paths) > 1 else "")
    testing = ""
    if body and "## Testing" in body:
        testing = re.split(r"\n(?:## |---)", body.split("## Testing", 1)[1], maxsplit=1)[0]
    # `@role` entries name the model that role runs (omp's modelRoles); Testing names models, not roles.
    models = [omp_config.expand_role(m) if m.startswith("@") else m for m in omp_config.load().behaviour_models]
    if models:
        missing = [m for m in models if not model_pattern(m).search(testing)]
        runs = " and ".join(models)
        unnamed = f"not named: {' or '.join(missing)}"
    else:
        named = [name for name, family in FAMILIES.items() if family.search(testing)]
        missing = [] if len(named) >= 2 else ["two families"]
        runs = "two models of different families (set models.behaviour in your config)"
        unnamed = f"named: {', '.join(named) or 'none'}"
    if missing:
        note("behavior", where, f"can this change what an agent does? then run a sample set on main and this branch "
                                f"with {runs}, scored from transcripts, and report it under ## Testing ({unnamed})")


def queue_manifest(cwd):
    """The queue manifest, fetched (rust-check records land from other hosts and CI); this host's copy when the
    fetch fails."""
    store = Store(cwd)
    try:
        return store.load()[0]
    except GitError:
        return store.load(fetch=False)[0]


def check_rust(diff, base, ref, cwd, manifest):
    """A Rust diff needs `rust-check` recorded on every platform it builds differently on: fork PRs
    get no CI before the merge and CI runs no Rust on PRs, so the maintainer's build was the first to compile
    #16202's Wayland change on Linux (E0618, bf9fd4bd6a) and to run clippy on #16140's macOS key
    reroute (ef7c45bd4b). A record counts for the Rust diff it checked (its patch id): a rebase or a
    CHANGELOG commit keeps it, a bot-round fix to a Rust line needs a new run."""
    platforms = rust_platforms(diff, base, ref, cwd)
    if not platforms:
        return
    diff_id = rust_diff_id(base, ref, cwd)
    done = rust_checks(manifest, diff_id)
    for platform in sorted(platforms):
        if platform in done:
            continue
        placement = omp_config.load().build[platform]
        how = rust_where(platform, placement)
        if how is None:
            note("rust", platform, f"unchecked: build.{platform} is \"{placement}\" in your config, so nothing "
                                   f"compiles this Rust diff on {platform} before the merge; say so in the PR's "
                                   "## Testing section")
        elif placement.startswith("ssh:") and not host_reachable(placement[4:]):
            note("rust", platform, f"unchecked: build.{platform} is \"{placement}\" but `ssh {placement[4:]}` does "
                                   f"not answer; run {how} once it does, or say in ## Testing that {platform} is "
                                   "unchecked")
        else:
            fix("rust", platform, f"no rust-check recorded for this Rust diff: run {how} "
                                  f"(build.{platform} = \"{placement}\"; CI compiles no Rust before the merge)")


def check_tests_env(added):
    """Credential code falls back to provider env vars (`keys.get`), so a token in the shell masks
    the stored row a new test sets up (#14753's CURSOR_ACCESS_TOKEN)."""
    if any(CREDENTIAL_SRC.match(p) for p in added) and any(is_test_path(p) for p in added):
        tests = sorted(p for p in added if is_test_path(p) and p.endswith(TS))
        note("env", ", ".join(tests)[:120],
             "credential lookups fall back to env keys: run these under `env -i HOME=$HOME PATH=$PATH bun test`")


def ox_bin(name, cwd):
    for root in (cwd, CHECKOUT):
        path = os.path.join(root, "node_modules", ".bin", name)
        if os.access(path, os.X_OK):
            return path
    return None


def check_tooling(added, ref, cwd):
    """oxlint and oxfmt over the files the diff adds TS lines to, read at `ref` so a PR branch
    needs no checkout, plus `new Promise(` in added code."""
    for path, lines in sorted(added.items()):
        if path.endswith(TS):
            for n, text in lines:
                if PROMISE.search(code_part(text)):
                    fix("promise", f"{path}:{n}", "use Promise.withResolvers() (AGENTS.md)")
    files = sorted(p for p in added if p.endswith(TS) and not p.endswith(".d.ts"))
    if not files:
        return
    lint, fmt = ox_bin("oxlint", cwd), ox_bin("oxfmt", cwd)
    if not lint or not fmt:
        note("tooling", cwd, "oxlint/oxfmt not installed: run `bun install` to enable the lint and format checks")
        return
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="precheck-") as tmp:
        for path in files + [".oxlintrc.json", ".oxfmtrc.json"]:
            blob = subprocess.run(["git", "show", f"{ref}:{path}"], capture_output=True, cwd=cwd)
            if blob.returncode == 0:
                os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(os.path.join(tmp, path)), exist_ok=True)
                with open(os.path.join(tmp, path), "wb") as f:
                    f.write(blob.stdout)
        present = [p for p in files if os.path.exists(os.path.join(tmp, p))]
        added_at = {p: {n for n, _ in added[p]} for p in present}
        out = run(lint, "-c", ".oxlintrc.json", "--format", "unix", *present, cwd=tmp).stdout
        for line in out.splitlines():
            m = OX_LINE.match(line)
            if m and int(m.group(2)) in added_at.get(m.group(1), ()):
                fix("lint", f"{m.group(1)}:{m.group(2)}", f"{m.group(3)[:110]}: fix it, or "
                    "`// oxlint-disable-next-line <rule> -- <why>` when intended")
        scoped = [p for p in present if FMT_SCOPE.match(p)]
        if scoped:
            out = run(fmt, "--check", *scoped, cwd=tmp)
            for path in scoped:
                if path in out.stdout + out.stderr:
                    fix("fmt", path, f"not oxfmt-formatted: bunx oxfmt {path}")


def declarations(lines):
    found = {}
    for text in lines:
        m = DECL.match(text)
        if m and m.group(1) not in NOT_DECL:
            found[m.group(1)] = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", m.group(2).strip())
    return found


def check_api(added, removed):
    """A changed parameter list on a package's source declaration (#14761 shipped one undeclared)."""
    for path in sorted(removed):
        if not (path.startswith("packages/") and "/src/" in path) or is_test_path(path):
            continue
        before = declarations(removed[path])
        after = declarations(t for _, t in added.get(path, []))
        for name, params in sorted(after.items()):
            if name in before and before[name] != params:
                note("signature", f"{path} {name}",
                     f"({before[name][:50]}) -> ({params[:50]}): if exported, add a ### Breaking Changes entry")


def check_removed_calls(diff):
    """Each call statement the diff removes from source whose callee no added line calls, so each
    drop is deliberate; a call kept with new arguments or moved is not listed. #15604's refactor
    removed `enable_web_accessibility(pid, &app);` from `window_records` with no replacement;
    the maintainer put it back (`e84a0a6f5c`)."""
    called = {name for path, lines in diff.added.items() if not is_test_path(path)
              for _, t in lines for name in CALLEE.findall(code_part(t))}
    for path, lines in sorted(diff.removed_at.items()):
        if not path.endswith(CODE) or is_test_path(path):
            continue
        rust = path.endswith(".rs")
        ends = STMT_END + ((")",) if path.endswith(".py") else ())
        for n, ctx, text in lines:
            code = code_part(text).rstrip()
            m = CALL_STMT.match(code)
            if (not m or m.group(1) in NOT_CALL or m.group(1) in called or not code.endswith(ends)
                    or NOT_CALL_SHAPE.search(code)
                    # Rust: a match arm, a variant or tuple-struct value (`Ok(…)`), an inline test module.
                    or (rust and ("=>" in code or m.group(1)[0].isupper() or TEST_MODULE.search(ctx)))):
                continue
            where = f" in {ctx[:50]}" if ctx else ""
            note("removed", f"{path}:{n}{where}", f"{text.strip()[:80]}  (dropped on purpose?)")


def check_scope(commits, title):
    """Commits scoped outside the PR title: CONTRIBUTING.md wants one logical change per PR. the maintainer
    removed #15414's `test(coding-agent)` screen-state notice test from a `fix(natives)` PR
    (`3fe61bb3da`). `commits` is [(sha, subject, [paths])]; commits touching only CHANGELOGs (the
    attribution commit) are left out."""
    wanted = scopes(title)
    if not wanted:
        return
    for sha, subject, paths in commits:
        own = scopes(subject)
        changed = [p for p in paths if not p.endswith("CHANGELOG.md")]
        if not own or own <= wanted or not changed:
            continue
        kind = "test-only commit" if all(is_test_path(p) for p in changed) else "commit"
        note("scope", f"{sha[:10]} {subject[:60]}",
             f"{kind} scoped {','.join(sorted(own - wanted))}, outside the title's "
             f"({','.join(sorted(wanted))}): part of this PR's one logical change?")


def scopes(subject):
    m = re.match(r"\w+\(([^)]*)\)!?:", subject or "")
    return {s.strip() for s in m.group(1).split(",") if s.strip()} if m else set()


def check_text_tests(added):
    """Tests that assert on the text of a script or source file instead of running it. #15599's
    cmux test checked that the page script contained `"body"`, so it passed without the script
    ever running; the maintainer's Chromium-backed test found the empty-`innerText` bug (`132e741411`)."""
    for path, lines in sorted(added.items()):
        if not is_test_path(path):
            continue
        for n, text in lines:
            if SCRIPT_ASSERT.search(text) or SOURCE_READ.search(text):
                note("text-test", f"{path}:{n}", "asserts on script or source text: run it (a real browser, "
                                                  "process or module) so the test fails when it misbehaves")


def expect_subjects(code):
    """[(subject, rest of the line)] for each `expect(subject)` in a line, read to its closing paren."""
    found = []
    for m in re.finditer(r"\bexpect\(", code):
        depth, i = 1, m.end()
        while i < len(code) and depth:
            depth += {"(": 1, ")": -1}.get(code[i], 0)
            i += 1
        if not depth:
            found.append((code[m.end():i - 1], code[i:]))
    return found


# Methods that reshape a text without naming it: `prompt.join("\n")` is still the prompt.
TEXT_METHODS = {"join", "trim", "trimStart", "trimEnd", "toString", "toLowerCase", "toUpperCase", "slice",
                "substring", "replace", "replaceAll", "normalize", "at"}


def subject_name(subject):
    """The last name an expect subject reads: `tool.description` -> description, `await
    buildSystemPrompt(opts)` -> buildSystemPrompt, `tools.find(t => t.x)!.prompt` -> prompt,
    `session.systemPrompt.join("\\n")` -> systemPrompt."""
    s = subject.strip().rstrip("!?")
    while s.endswith(")"):
        depth, i = 0, len(s) - 1
        while i >= 0:
            depth += {")": 1, "(": -1}.get(s[i], 0)
            if not depth:
                break
            i -= 1
        s = s[:max(i, 0)].rstrip().rstrip("!?.")
        m = re.search(r"\??\.\s*([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)$", s)
        if m and m.group(1) in TEXT_METHODS:
            s = s[:m.start()].rstrip("!?")
    names = re.findall(r"[A-Za-z_$][\w$]*", s)
    return names[-1] if names else ""


def case_blocks(text):
    """[(first line, [lines])] of each test case in a test file's text: from its `it(`/`test(` line to
    the next one (or the end)."""
    lines = text.splitlines()
    starts = [i for i, line in enumerate(lines) if TEST_CASE.match(line)]
    return [(s + 1, lines[s:e]) for s, e in zip(starts, starts[1:] + [len(lines)])]


def prompt_only_session(block):
    """Whether a test case builds a full agent session and asserts only `toContain`/`toMatch` on
    something named description, prompt or instructions."""
    code = [code_part(line) for line in block]
    if not any(FULL_SESSION.search(c) for c in code):
        return False
    asserts = [(s, rest) for c in code for s, rest in expect_subjects(c)]
    return bool(asserts) and all(TEXT_MATCHER.match(rest) and PROMPT_NAME.search(subject_name(s)) for s, rest in asserts)


def check_test_kinds(added, ref, cwd):
    """Test lines of kinds maintainers deleted from our merged PRs while keeping the code: a spy on or
    mock of the repo logger (debug-log wording, #16579's `spyOn(logger, "debug")`, `c98d4561f0`); an
    assertion that a `src/prompts/` file's text appears in a description, prompt or request; and a
    test case that builds a full `createAgentSession` only to find prompt text (#16918's SDK test,
    `4b13432524`). A unit test of a description builder checking a section in or out is not one."""
    for path, lines in sorted(added.items()):
        if not is_test_path(path):
            continue
        whole = file_at(ref, path, cwd) if path.endswith(TS) else ""
        bound = {g for line in whole.splitlines() if (m := PROMPT_BINDING.match(line)) for g in m.groups() if g}
        for n, text in lines:
            code = code_part(text)
            if not code.strip():
                continue
            if LOGGER_TEST.search(code):
                note("log-test", f"{path}:{n}", f"spies on or mocks the logger: log wording is no contract a user "
                                                f"notices; assert what the code does ({COUNT_RULE})")
            if any(TEXT_MATCHER.match(rest) for _, rest in expect_subjects(code)) and (
                    PROMPT_FILE.search(text) or any(re.search(rf"(?<![\w$]){re.escape(b)}(?![\w$])", code) for b in bound)):
                note("prompt-test", f"{path}:{n}", f"asserts a prompt file's text appears in a description, prompt or "
                                                   f"request: that pins wording, not behaviour; drop it ({COUNT_RULE})")
        new = {n for n, _ in lines}
        for start, block in case_blocks(whole):
            if start in new and prompt_only_session(block):
                note("prompt-test", f"{path}:{start}", f"builds a full agent session only to find prompt or description "
                                                       f"text: test the builder's branch in a unit test, or drop it "
                                                       f"({COUNT_RULE})")


def each_rows(texts):
    """Rows of the `it.each([...])`/`test.each([...])` table opening at texts[0] (texts: it and the
    added lines after it); 1 when the table is not an inline array."""
    code = " ".join(code_part(t) for t in texts)
    start = code.find("(", code.find(".each")) + 1
    rest = code[start:].lstrip()
    if not rest.startswith("["):
        return 1
    depth, commas, prev = 0, 0, ""
    for ch in rest:
        if ch in "([{":
            depth += 1
        elif ch in ")]}":
            depth -= 1
            if not depth:
                break
        elif ch == "," and depth == 1:
            commas += 1
        if not ch.isspace():
            prev = ch
    return commas if prev in ",[" else commas + 1


def is_counted_source(path):
    """Shipped source for the test-volume count: code, catalog KDL, and markdown under src/ (prompts)."""
    return not is_test_path(path) and (path.endswith(CODE + (".kdl",)) or ("/src/" in path and path.endswith(".md")))


def count_tests(diff, ref, cwd):
    """(added test lines, added source lines, added test cases, changed source files), blank lines
    left out, and fixtures, snapshots, generated files, lock files, CHANGELOGs and docs counted as
    neither. A Rust file's inline test module (below its `#[cfg(test)] mod`) counts as tests."""
    tests = source = cases = 0
    files = {p for p in diff.removed_at if is_counted_source(p) and not NOT_COUNTED.search(p)}
    for path, lines in diff.added.items():
        if NOT_COUNTED.search(path) or not (path.endswith(CODE) or is_counted_source(path)):
            continue
        start = rust_test_start(path, ref, cwd) if path.endswith(".rs") and not is_test_path(path) else -1
        for i, (n, text) in enumerate(lines):
            if not text.strip():
                continue
            if is_test_path(path) or 0 < start <= n + 1:  # the `#[cfg(test)]` line above the module too
                tests += 1
                if EACH_CASE.match(text):
                    run_end = next((j for j in range(i + 1, len(lines)) if lines[j][0] != n + j - i), len(lines))
                    cases += each_rows([t for _, t in lines[i:min(run_end, i + 300)]])
                elif TEST_CASE.match(text):
                    cases += 1
            else:
                source += 1
                files.add(path)
    return tests, source, cases, len(files)


def check_test_volume(diff, ref, cwd):
    """More test than the change needs (thresholds above). H4vC pared same-path variants from 7 of
    our PRs in three days (`642fce6b03` #17490, `3587ec7f14` #17510, `0fa4a8a570` #16309, #16552,
    #16595, #17766, #17772); our ratio ran 1.0-3.6 test lines per source line, his 1.0."""
    tests, source, cases, files = count_tests(diff, ref, cwd)
    over = []
    if source and tests - TEST_LINE_RATIO * source >= TEST_LINE_EXCESS:
        over.append(f"{tests} test lines to {source} source lines (over {TEST_LINE_RATIO:g}x by "
                    f"{tests - TEST_LINE_RATIO * source:.0f})")
    if cases > CASES_PER_FILE * max(1, files):
        over.append(f"{cases} test cases for {files} changed source file(s) (over {CASES_PER_FILE} each)")
    if over:
        note("tests", "; ".join(over), f"for each test, name the branch it reaches that no other test in the PR "
                                       f"reaches; merge or delete the rest ({COUNT_RULE})")


def check_consumers(diff, ref, cwd):
    """A field added to an interface or object type, against source files that name a third of
    that file's fields but not the new one: field-by-field readers, copiers and allowlists. The
    session anonymizer keeps only fields it has a rule for, so #15680's new `runtime` entry field
    was dropped from anonymized exports until the maintainer added a rule (`71da31fb7d`)."""
    new = {}
    for path, lines in diff.added.items():
        if not path.endswith(TS) or is_test_path(path) or "/src/" not in path:
            continue
        for n, text in lines:
            m = FIELD_DECL.match(code_part(text))
            if m and TYPE_CTX.search(diff.added_in.get((path, n), "")):
                new.setdefault(path, set()).add(m.group(1))
    for path, fields in sorted(new.items()):
        names = set(FIELD_NAME.findall(run("git", "show", f"{ref}:{path}", cwd=cwd).stdout)) - fields
        names = {n for n in names if len(n) >= 6 and DOC_NAME.search(n)}
        if len(names) < CONSUMER_MIN_FIELDS:
            continue
        need = max(6, -(-len(names) // 3))
        word = re.compile(r"\b(%s)\b" % "|".join(sorted(names)))
        out = run("git", "grep", "-l", "-w", "-E", "|".join(sorted(names)), ref, "--",
                  "packages/*/src/*.ts", "packages/*/src/**/*.ts", cwd=cwd).stdout
        for hit in out.splitlines():
            other = hit.split(":", 1)[-1]
            if other == path:
                continue
            source = run("git", "show", f"{ref}:{other}", cwd=cwd).stdout
            missing = sorted(f for f in fields if not re.search(rf"\b{re.escape(f)}\b", source))
            if missing and len(set(word.findall(source))) >= need:
                note("consumer", other, f"names {len(set(word.findall(source)))} fields of {path.rsplit('/', 1)[-1]} "
                                        f"but not new {', '.join(missing)}: does it read, copy or allowlist them?")


def check_docs(added, removed, contexts, ref, cwd):
    """Docs naming a function the diff changes (#14751, #14659: docs kept the old contract)."""
    names = set()
    for path, ctx in contexts.items():
        if path.endswith(TS) and not is_test_path(path) and "/src/" in path:
            for c in ctx:
                m = CONTEXT_NAME.search(c)
                names |= {g for g in (m.groups() if m else ()) if g and g not in NOT_DECL}
            names |= set(declarations(t for _, t in added.get(path, []))) | set(declarations(removed.get(path, [])))
    names = {n for n in names if len(n) >= 6 and DOC_NAME.search(n)}
    hits = {}
    for name in sorted(names):
        out = run("git", "grep", "-l", "-w", "-F", "-e", name, ref, "--", *DOC_GLOBS, cwd=cwd).stdout
        for line in out.splitlines():
            doc = line.split(":", 1)[-1]
            if doc not in added and doc not in removed:
                hits.setdefault(doc, []).append(name)
    for doc, found in sorted(hits.items()):
        note("docs", doc, f"mentions {', '.join(found[:4])}; update it if the contract it states changed")


def unreleased_span(path, ref, cwd):
    text = run("git", "show", f"{ref}:{path}", cwd=cwd).stdout.splitlines()
    start = next((i + 1 for i, l in enumerate(text) if l.startswith("## [Unreleased]")), None)
    if start is None:
        return None
    end = next((i + 1 for i, l in enumerate(text[start:], start) if l.startswith("## [")), len(text) + 1)
    return start, end


def unreleased_code(diff, base, ref, cwd):
    """{package: ["<sha> <subject>", ...]}: the commits that wrote source lines the diff removes or
    rewrites in that package and that no `v*` tag ships without `ref` (`git tag --contains C
    --no-contains ref`, so a release that already holds this fix does not count). A bug that came
    with such a commit is a regression no user saw. #15941 fixed a line #14591 wrote after v18.11.1,
    and the maintainer dropped its CHANGELOG entry (`7c9d457d70`). Over our 109 merges 2026-10-05..16 this
    names a commit on 9 PRs; on the other 8 the bug was older than the lines it rewrote."""
    commits = blamed_commits(diff, base, cwd)
    shipped, fresh = {}, {}
    for pkg, shas in sorted(commits.items()):
        for sha in sorted(shas):
            if sha not in shipped:
                shipped[sha] = bool(run("git", "tag", "--list", "v*", "--contains", sha, "--no-contains", ref,
                                        cwd=cwd).stdout.split())
            if not shipped[sha]:
                subject = run("git", "log", "-1", "--format=%s", sha, cwd=cwd).stdout.strip()
                fresh.setdefault(pkg, []).append(f"{sha[:10]} {subject[:60].rstrip()}")
    return fresh


def check_changelogs(added, ref, cwd, number, fresh=None):
    """`fresh` is `unreleased_code`'s result. AGENTS.md wants user-facing entries, and a fix of a
    regression no release shipped changes nothing a user saw: the maintainer dropped #15941's (`7c9d457d70`).
    So where the diff rewrites unreleased code, a missing entry is a NOTE, not a FIX."""
    fresh = fresh or {}
    for pkg in sorted({p for p in map(package_of, added) if p}):
        log = f"{pkg}/CHANGELOG.md"
        entries = [(n, t) for n, t in added.get(log, []) if t.strip().startswith("- ")]
        written = f"rewrites lines no release ships yet, from {'; '.join(fresh.get(pkg, [])[:3])}"
        if not entries:
            if pkg in fresh:
                note("changelog", log, f"no entry; {written}: right if the bug came with that code; add one if "
                                       "the last release shows it")
            else:
                fix("changelog", log, "no added entry for this touched package")
            continue
        if pkg in fresh:
            note("changelog", log, f"{written}: if the bug came with that code, no user saw it, so drop the entry "
                                   "(7c9d457d70); keep it if the last release shows the bug")
        span = unreleased_span(log, ref, cwd)
        for n, text in entries:
            if not span or not span[0] < n < span[1]:
                fix("changelog", f"{log}:{n}", "entry is outside ## [Unreleased]")
            elif number and (f"/pull/{number}" not in text or f"@{ME}" not in text):
                fix("changelog", f"{log}:{n}", f"entry lacks the #{number} link and @{ME} credit")


def check_drift(ref, cwd):
    """Placement after merging origin/main, which is where the maintainer reads it (every maintainer
    review on 2026-10-08 opened with "the CHANGELOG entry has to go under ## [Unreleased]")."""
    for path, entry, section in changelog_drift(ref, cwd=cwd):
        fix("changelog", path, f"lands under {section} once origin/main merges in: rebase onto it and move "
                               f"the entry under ## [Unreleased] ({entry[2:60]}…)")


def prose_length(body):
    """Characters a reader reads as prose: tables, <details>, images, videos and the checklist don't count.
    An image stands in for a table, which is free too: its markup (URLs, alt) is not prose, and neither are
    the blank lines that set it apart (runs of blank lines left where a block was count as one). A video is
    a `user-attachments` URL on a line of its own, which GitHub renders as its player."""
    prose = re.sub(r"<details>.*?</details>|<picture>.*?</picture>|<img\b[^>]*>|!\[[^\]]*\]\([^)]*\)", "",
                   body, flags=re.S)
    prose = "\n".join(l for l in prose.splitlines()
                      if not l.lstrip().startswith(("|", "- [ ]", "- [x]", "- [X]"))
                      and not re.fullmatch(r"\s*https://github\.com/user-attachments/assets/[0-9a-f-]{36}\s*", l))
    return len(re.sub(r"\n\s*\n(\s*\n)+", "\n\n", prose))


def check_body(body, ours=(), own_words=None):
    """Body checks; `ours` is the numbers of our open PRs, for the "Builds on #N" merge-order check;
    `own_words` the sentences `contrib record own-words` holds for the branch (None: no record)."""
    for heading in ("## What", "## Why", "## Testing"):
        if heading not in body:
            fix("body", heading, "heading missing")
        elif not re.sub(r"<!--.*?-->", "", body.split(heading, 1)[1].split("\n## ", 1)[0], flags=re.S).strip():
            fix("body", heading, "section is empty")
    what = omp_flow.what_paragraph(body)
    if own_words is not None:
        if not omp_flow.own_words_match(body, own_words):
            fix("own-words", "## What", "the first paragraph is not the user's recorded own-words sentence: put it "
                                        "there verbatim (`contrib body`), never a substitute"
                                        + ("" if own_words else "; none is recorded yet: ask the user (contrib next)"))
    elif not re.search(r"\b(I|I'm|I've|I'd|[Mm]y|[Mm]e|[Ww]e|[Oo]ur)\b", what):
        fix("body", "## What", "first paragraph has no first-person own-words sentence")
    prose = prose_length(body)
    if prose > BODY_LIMIT:
        fix("body", "length", f"{prose} chars outside tables, <details> and checklist (limit {BODY_LIMIT})")
    for m in sorted(set(MENTIONS.findall(body))):
        fix("mention", f"@{m}", "no @-mentions of Codex, roboomp or maintainers")
    for line in merge_order_lines(body, ours):
        fix("order", line[:90], "merge-order text: a PR that needs another waits in the queue until it merges "
                                "(shaping.md, Open-PR rule); a related PR goes under Why as `Related: #N`")


def check_siblings(ref, number, cwd, branch=None):
    """Another open PR's commits on this branch, and code conflicts with our open ready PRs once both
    land on origin/main (shaping.md, Open-PR rule). The queue manifest supplies recorded needs and
    bases, so a child stacked on this branch's older commits is not read as commits it carries."""
    q = Queue(cwd, Store(cwd).load()[0], GitHub())
    item = q.adhoc(ref, number, branch or None)
    remedy = (f"pr-queue requeue {number} --needs {{}}" if number in q.by_number
              else "pr-queue add <branch> --needs {}")
    for other, count in sorted(((o, n) for o, n in q.carried(item).items() if o != "main"),
                               key=lambda x: x[0].sort_id):
        fix("carries", other.label, f"{count} of its commits are on this branch: it waits in the queue until "
                                    f"{other.label} merges ({remedy.format(other.label)})")
    if q.carried(item).get("main"):
        note("carries", "main", f"{q.carried(item)['main']} commit(s) main already has under other SHAs: rebase "
                                "drops them")
    for label, code, generated, accepted in q.conflicts_with_ready(item):
        if code and accepted:
            # A P0/P1 opening over an old P2 (shaping.md, Open-PR rule 3).
            note("sibling", label, f"code conflict in {', '.join(code)}: {accepted}")
        elif code:
            fix("sibling", label, f"code conflict in {', '.join(code)}: one of the two waits in the queue "
                                  "(pr-queue plan says which)")
        else:
            note("sibling", label, f"only the generated {', '.join(generated)} conflict: regenerated at merge")
    sharing = {}
    for other, units in q.sharing(item, q.ready_items()):
        for unit in units:
            sharing.setdefault(unit, []).append(other.label)
    for unit, labels in sorted(sharing.items()):
        note("build", unit, f"also changed by open ready {', '.join(labels)}: build and test the merged tree with "
                            f"{'it' if len(labels) == 1 else 'them'} (queue.md, Opening)")
    return set(q.by_number)


def check_links(pr):
    body = pr["body"] or ""
    closing = {c["number"] for c in pr["closingIssuesReferences"]}
    mentioned = {int(number) for match in re.findall(
        r"(?<![\w/#])#(?![0-9a-fA-F]{6}\b)([1-9]\d{2,})\b|/issues/([1-9]\d{2,})\b", body)
        for number in match if number} - closing - {pr["number"]}
    for n in sorted(mentioned):
        item = gh_json("api", f"repos/{REPO}/issues/{n}")
        if "pull_request" not in item and item["state"] == "open":
            note("link", f"#{n}", f"open issue mentioned but not closed: Fixes or Related? ({item['title'][:70]})")
    for n in sorted(closing):
        note("closes", f"#{n}", "closes on merge; confirm nothing it asks for remains")


def branch_commits(base, ref, cwd):
    """[(sha, subject, [paths])] of the non-merge commits in base..ref, oldest first."""
    out = run("git", "log", "--no-merges", "--reverse", "--format=%x00%H%x1f%s", "--name-only",
              f"{base}..{ref}", cwd=cwd).stdout
    commits = []
    for chunk in out.split("\x00")[1:]:
        head, _, files = chunk.partition("\n")
        sha, _, subject = head.partition("\x1f")
        commits.append((sha, subject, [f for f in files.splitlines() if f]))
    return commits


def check_refs(base, ref, cwd):
    """Commit messages naming a PR as `#N` (or `owner/repo#N`, a PR URL): each push of such a commit's
    SHA lands a "referenced this pull request" event on that PR. A reword is a push of its own, so
    it waits for the next rebase the PR needs anyway, which rewrites them with `quiet-refs`."""
    out = run("git", "log", "--no-merges", "--reverse", "--format=%x00%H%x1f%s%x1f%B", f"{base}..{ref}",
              cwd=cwd).stdout
    for chunk in out.split("\x00")[1:]:
        sha, subject, message = chunk.split("\x1f", 2)
        if quiet_refs(message) != message:
            note("refs", sha[:10], f"{subject}: names a PR as #N; after the next rebase it needs anyway, "
                                   "run scripts/quiet-refs before that push (never a push of its own)")


def check_queued(pr):
    """A closed PR whose change waits in the queue reopens with the stored body, not GitHub's; and while
    the opening pause is set, a draft or unopened PR does not turn ready or open (queue.md)."""
    manifest = Store(CHECKOUT).load()[0]
    entry = stored_body_entry(manifest, pr["number"], pr["state"])
    if entry:
        fix("queue", stored_body_message(pr["number"], entry))
    pause = opening_pause(manifest)
    if pause and (pr["state"] == "CLOSED" or (pr["state"] == "OPEN" and pr["isDraft"])):
        fix("queue", f"#{pr['number']} is {'a draft' if pr['state'] == 'OPEN' else 'closed'}: {pause_line(pause)}")


def main(argv):
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(usage=__doc__.split("\n\n", 2)[1].removeprefix("usage: "))
    parser.add_argument("number", nargs="?", type=int)
    parser.add_argument("--ref")
    parser.add_argument("--body")
    parser.add_argument("--number", dest="link_number", type=int)
    parser.add_argument("--no-siblings", action="store_true")
    parser.add_argument("--base", default="origin/main")
    parser.add_argument("--title")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    pr = None
    if args.number:
        pr = gh_json("pr", "view", str(args.number), "-R", REPO, "--json",
                     "number,state,isDraft,title,headRefName,body,closingIssuesReferences")
        check_queued(pr)
        cwd = CHECKOUT
        run("git", "fetch", "-q", STORE_REMOTE, pr["headRefName"], cwd=cwd)
        run("git", "fetch", "-q", "origin", "main", cwd=cwd)
        ref = f"{STORE_REMOTE}/{pr['headRefName']}"
    else:
        cwd, ref = os.getcwd(), args.ref or "HEAD"
        if run("git", "rev-parse", "--git-dir", cwd=cwd).returncode != 0:
            sys.exit(__doc__)
    number = pr["number"] if pr else args.link_number
    base = run("git", "merge-base", args.base, ref, cwd=cwd).stdout.strip()
    if not base:
        sys.exit(f"no merge base between {args.base} and {ref}")
    diff = Diff(base, ref, cwd)
    added, removed, contexts = diff.added, diff.removed, diff.contexts
    check_code(added, base, cwd)
    check_timing(added, ref, cwd)
    check_policy(added)
    check_tests_env(added)
    check_api(added, removed)
    check_docs(added, removed, contexts, ref, cwd)
    check_removed_calls(diff)
    check_text_tests(added)
    check_test_kinds(added, ref, cwd)
    check_test_volume(diff, ref, cwd)
    check_consumers(diff, ref, cwd)
    check_prompt_values(added, ref, cwd)
    check_rust(diff, base, ref, cwd, queue_manifest(cwd))
    commits = branch_commits(base, ref, cwd)
    check_scope(commits, pr["title"] if pr else args.title or (commits[0][1] if commits else ""))
    check_refs(base, ref, cwd)
    if not all(re.match(r"test(\([^)]*\))?!?:", subject) for _, subject, _ in commits):
        check_changelogs(added, ref, cwd, number, unreleased_code(diff, base, ref, cwd))
    check_tooling(added, ref, cwd)
    check_drift(ref, cwd)
    if pr:
        branch = pr["headRefName"]
    elif args.ref:
        remote = re.escape(STORE_REMOTE)
        branch = re.sub(rf"^(refs/remotes/{remote}/|refs/heads/|{remote}/)", "", args.ref)
    else:
        branch = run("git", "symbolic-ref", "--short", "-q", "HEAD", cwd=cwd).stdout.strip()
    if branch:
        check_review(branch, cwd)
    ours = set() if args.no_siblings else check_siblings(ref, number, cwd, branch)
    body = pr["body"] if pr else (open(args.body).read() if args.body else None)
    if body is not None:
        check_body(body or "", ours - {number}, None if pr else omp_flow.own_words_for(cwd, branch))
    check_behavior_eval(added, body)
    if pr:
        check_links(pr)
    elif branch and run("git", "show-ref", "-q", "--verify", f"refs/heads/{branch}", cwd=cwd).returncode == 0:
        omp_flow.note_precheck(cwd, branch, run("git", "rev-parse", ref, cwd=cwd).stdout.strip(), not findings, body,
                               args.body)
    sys.exit(1 if findings else 0)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main(sys.argv[1:])
